.TH OBJCOPY09 1 2026-11-11 "bcc09 0.14.0" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
objcopy09 \- make a 6809 executable into what a machine loads
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B objcopy09
.RB [ \-O
.IR format ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-D
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR revision ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.I executable output
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B objcopy09
reads an executable (an a.out file) from
.BR ld09 (1)
and writes its code and data in one of the forms that 6809 machines,
their systems and their tools load.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-O " format"
The form to write:
.RS
.TP
.B binary
The bytes alone (the default): the code, then the data (with
.BR \-p ,
each at its place).
.TP
.B srec
Motorola S-records: S0 (holding the name), S1, and S9 with the entry
point.
.TP
.B ihex
Intel hex records, with CR LF line ends.
.TP
.B decb
A Disk Extended Color BASIC file, for LOADM (and EXEC): blocks of an
address and bytes, a second for data linked apart from the code, and
the entry point.
.TP
.B ddos
A DragonDOS binary file (code and data together: one block, so data
linked apart from the code needs
.BR \-p ).
.TP
.B cas
A Color BASIC cassette image, for CLOADM: a name block (the name, up to
8 characters, and a machine-language file's entry point and load
address), data blocks, and an end block, each after a leader.
For the CoCo and the Dragon, and for emulators (XRoar's
.BR \-load\-tape ).
A machine-language file is one block of memory, so data linked apart
from the code needs
.BR \-p .
.TP
.B wav
The same, as the sound a cassette recorder plays to the machine
(43200 samples a second).
.TP
.B os9
An OS-9 program module: the module header (type $11, a 6809 program),
the code, the image of the data (which the start-up code of
.B "bcc09 \-Mo"
copies to the data area), the name, and the CRC.
The data area the module asks for holds the program's data and BSS
and then
.B \-d
bytes for the stack.
The executable's data should be linked at 0
.RB ( "ld09 \-i" ,
as
.B "bcc09 \-Mo"
does): there is a warning if it isn't.
.TP
.B flex
A FLEX binary (.CMD) file, a FLEX command: $02 records of an address,
a count (up to 255) and bytes, and a $16 record with the entry point.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
The name: of the cassette file, the OS-9 module, or the S0 record.
By default the output file's name, in upper case.
.TP
.BI \-T " address"
The code's load address (by default the address it was linked at).
.TP
.BI \-D " address"
The data's load address, for data linked apart from the code (by
default the address it was linked at).
.TP
.B \-p
With
.BR binary ,
.BR ddos ,
.B cas
or
.BR wav ,
for a program whose data isn't just after its code (above it or below
it), write one image, from the lower of their addresses to the end of
the higher, the gap between them zeros: so each is at its address's
place, and the file loads at the lower address (the entry point is
still the program's).
Code and data that overlap, or span more than 64K, are an error.
.TP
.BI \-v " revision"
With
.BR os9 ,
the module's revision, the low 4 bits of its header's attribute and
revision byte (0 to 15; up to 127 is taken, setting attribute bits
too).
.TP
.BI \-d " size"
With
.BR os9 ,
the bytes of the data area for the stack (by default the size of
.BR "ld09 \-H" ,
or 1024).
.TP
.B \-r
With
.BR os9 ,
mark the module reentrant (it is anyway if its data is at 0, or it has
none).
.PP
Addresses and sizes are decimal, hexadecimal after
.B 0x
or
.BR $ ,
or octal after a 0, and must fit in 16 bits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if the file was written, 1 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
A Color BASIC program on a cassette, a FLEX command and an OS-9 module:
.PP
.RS
.nf
objcopy09 \-O cas \-n HELLO hello hello.cas
objcopy09 \-O flex hello.out HELLO.CMD
objcopy09 \-O os9 \-n hello \-d 2048 hello.out hello
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR ld09 (1),
.BR objdump09 (1)
